[{:seon.task/name "the-unit-tap-reports-every-macro-expanded"
  :seon.task/plan "Shrink brief 3, row 16 (worktree session SHRINK2, 2026-10-23, read on main 8a5818c): seon.program/macro-uses (src/seon/program.clj:282-292) re-reads each form's text and resolves every list head in its namespace to find the macros it calls, because the analyzed node is the expansion and names no macro. That reading can disagree with what the compiler did: a local named like a macro, a quoted list, a head shadowed by a binding (AGENTS §9: no reading of code where the compiler's facts exist). Cause, in Jolt: jolt-core/jolt/analyzer.clj:2333-2342 expands a macro call knowing its var (macro-symbol ctx head) and records it only in *expansion-box* (:120), a diagnostic box holding the innermost expansion, reset as analysis returns; nothing reports it to the unit tap (:121-, jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*), so the tap's consumer (seon.program/tap) cannot know which macros a unit expanded. Correct, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our Jolt: at that expansion site, when a unit is being tapped, add the macro's qualified symbol to the unit's set of expanded macros (every expansion, nested ones included), and hand that set to the unit tap with the form and node (a third argument, or a key on the node's unit info). Then seon.program/tap records it in the account (:macros), seon.program/dependents reads it, and macro-uses and its re-read go. Proofs: a Jolt test that a unit expanding a macro which expands another reports both; seon.program-test: a form whose local shadows a macro's name records no macro use (red today through macro-uses), and dependents of a changed macro are unchanged otherwise."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "analysis-allocates-the-ir-not-its-lookups"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23; Jolt jolt/analysis-lookups 4af66adf on jolt/record-field-binds 0d39edc3). Seon's import of src and test analyzes ~2,400 forms, ~442k IR nodes; on 195 real forms analysis allocated 2,907 B a node, about 25x the IR it built. Causes, each fixed where it costs: (1) host-contract.ss hc-unchecked-math? and hc-invoke-rewriter, read on every list form, built an \"ns/name\" string and probed the var table inside a guard: they find the var cell once and deref it; (2) hc-expand-1 walked the whole call form on every macro call (hc-macro-arg, turning the reader's set forms into sets), a list cell per node even when nothing changed, so nested macros were quadratic (100 nested whens 1.69 MB, 200 5.28 MB): the analyzer scans each top-level form once (form-holds-set-form?, allocating nothing) and a form holding none expands without the walk (form-expand-1's normalize?), since a macro only builds real sets; (3) a var reference resolved its symbol three times and a macro call its head four: resolve-global answers :macro and the macro arm resolves once; (4) add-locals updated :hints and :jprims for every binding, and every macro call rebuilt &env from every local in scope: both change only what is there, &env kept in env. Bytes a unit (Jolt test/analyze_alloc_test.clj, make analyzealloc): an (inc ...) level 4,536 -> 2,241, a var reference 889 -> 601, a when inside eight locals 63,076 (quadratic) -> 4,689, a let binding 1,742 -> 2,565 (it carries &env now). Seon's import (with tree-seq's fix): 479M -> 366M ticks, 2,974 -> 2,088 MB warm. What remains is the macros' own expansion (clojure.core's macro code) and the IR itself."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-missing-var-through-an-alias-says-no-such-var-in-any-namespace"
  :seon.task/plan "An alias-qualified call to a var that does not exist (reader/delimiters, with seon.reader required :as reader and delimiters not yet written) answers \"Unknown class reader\" when the call runs while another namespace is current, as every test does under bin/test (md-scan's delims bench, 2026-10-31: seon.reader-test's delimiters tests on jolt/bench-delims), and \"No such var: reader/delimiters\" when the current namespace is the one the form was compiled in (a REPL; root's eval answers it right). Cause, in Jolt (reference-code/jolt at 4df132c3): the analyzer emits such a call as a host static on the alias as written (jolt-core/jolt/analyzer.clj, the `ns` branch of the symbol analysis: `(host-static ns nm)`, the late-bound arm a loaded namespace missing the var keeps on purpose), and at the miss host/chez/java/host-static.ss's static-miss-message resolves that alias against (chez-current-ns), the namespace current at RUN time, not the one the call was compiled in; in another namespace the alias is unknown, the name falls to unknown-class-message. Correct, as the JVM: \"No such var: reader/delimiters\" (the alias as written; test/chez/unit.edn:243 pins \"No such var: s/no-such-fn\") wherever the call runs. Two ways, the owner's to choose (a change to a dependency, AGENTS section 3): (a) the analyzer emits the host static with the namespace the alias names in the compile ns (jolt.host/namespace-for, which already answers it) and the written spelling for the message (the IR's :host-static and backend_scheme.clj carry one more field to the site); or (b) the emitted site carries its compile namespace and static-miss-message resolves the alias there. (a) also fixes a late-bound aliased host reference's success path, which today resolves the alias at run time too. Not a one-line rewrite of the class to the full name: that changes the message's spelling from Clojure's. Proofs: a Jolt unit case calling (s/no-such-fn 1) from a fn compiled in one namespace and run with another current; on Seon, seon.repl-test (or my.test world) calling an aliased missing var from a test namespace under bin/test answers my.agent/no-var's answer. The pin bump runs every Jolt gate (docs/forks.md#jolt). Measured 2026-11-05, reproduced by a scratch test on seon.program-test's helpers (not kept; the proof to add below is it): this is also why a proof written before its function reads a stale red, so (a) is the choice. The account records the host static as written, so the test form's :form/refs holds hr/h, not app.hr/h: defining app.hr/h moves no digest and my.agent/tests-reaching answers []. Evaluating the same text again adds app.hr/h and leaves hr/h (the digest moves), but seon.program/run-digest reads forms' entities and no refs, so seon.prove answers the finished run (\"asked again after its run, nothing it reads changed\"). An agent on vars-history lost its last 15 of 40 turns to this, three proves answering ran 0, reused 1; the verdict cache holds 27 such reds across five groups of tests since 2026-10-31. Under (a) the host static names the namespace, so the first record's refs are right and the digest moves at the definition, and the body compiled before the function existed finds it. Writing the proof first stays as the docs say. Proof to add: seon.program-test, a test recorded before what it calls moves its digest when that is defined and reaches it, with no second record of the test."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-missing-var-through-an-alias-is-no-such-var-in-a-test}}]
